#
# Hand-authored (2026-08-21, adversarial-review fix round) -- NOT a
# generator stub. emit_oracles() never overwrites this file once it exists
# (specs/SCHEMA.md §8.2 rule 7), so edits here are safe across
# regeneration.
#
# Scenario:   lambda-log-group-ownership-and-retention (specs/lambda-log-group-ownership-and-retention.yaml)
# Intent doc: oracles/lambda-log-group-ownership-and-retention/intent.md
# Graded against the awscdk arm's synthesized CloudFormation template
# (cdk.out/ScenarioStack.template.json) -- specs/SCHEMA.md §4.2/§8.
#
# THIS FILE WAS PREVIOUSLY A DELIBERATE generator-scaffolded placeholder
# (the marker `is_stub_policy()` greps for, spelled out fully in
# tests/_assert_lib.sh's own docstring rather than repeated verbatim here
# on purpose -- writing it literally in this comment would itself trip
# that same stub-detection grep) (2026-08-21,
# initial authoring), on the theory that catch 4 (`log-group-name-
# diverges-from-function`) needed STRING CONCATENATION to check --
# "LogGroupName == '/aws/lambda/' + FunctionName" -- which cfn-guard 3.2.0
# genuinely cannot do (no string-building operator at all). That theory
# was CORRECT for the naming-convention path alone, but an adversarial
# review found the underlying oracle itself was wrong: this scenario's
# real fact is "the declared log group governs this function's logs",
# which has TWO independent, sufficient mechanisms -- exact convention-name
# match, OR explicit `LoggingConfig.LogGroup` wiring (a first-class Lambda
# argument, GA'd 2023-11-16, that accepts a log group of ANY name at all;
# `aws-cdk-lib` 2.263.0's own `lambda.Function.logGroup` prop compiles
# straight to it -- `function.js::getLoggingConfig`:
# `logGroup: props.logGroup?.logGroupRef.logGroupName`, landing at
# `CfnFunction.Properties.LoggingConfig.LogGroup`). The WIRING disjunct
# needs no string concatenation at all -- it is a plain structural
# EXISTS check -- so it is fully implementable in cfn-guard with no
# capability gap. Only the naming disjunct's EXACT-suffix form remains
# unimplementable here (see "RESIDUAL GAP" below); the weaker PREFIX form
# still is. specs/lambda-log-group-ownership-and-retention.yaml's own
# header comment ("VERIFIER-REJECTED FIX" / "RESIDUAL, NARROWER
# TOOL-CAPABILITY GAP") carries the full evidence trail, including the
# hand-built PASS/FAIL transcripts this rule was checked against before
# being written into the task tree.
#
# Encodes catch 4 (`log-group-name-diverges-from-function`, graph-dependency)
# and its own tier-1 structural_assert spec
# (`log-group-governs-the-function-cfn`, specs/lambda-log-group-
# ownership-and-retention.yaml): a log group that only matches the
# `/aws/lambda/...` NAMING PATTERN (not necessarily this exact function's
# own name) governs nothing UNLESS the function is also explicitly wired
# to it. `deny` (i.e. non-compliant) fires only when NEITHER disjunct
# holds for a Lambda function in the template.
#
# `some %log_groups { ... } or some %functions { ... }` is cfn-guard 3's
# real boolean-OR operator between two `some` blocks at a rule's top
# level -- same construct as
# oracles/cfn-guard/asg-launch-template-tag-propagation/policy.guard's own
# `instance_tag_costcenter_reaches`/`instance_tag_environment_reaches`
# rules (that file's own header comment names this as the precedent). A
# custom `<<...>> ` violation message attached directly after such an
# `or`-joined pair was found, empirically, to break cfn-guard 3.2.0's
# parser (confirmed directly, 2026-08-21 -- a `<<...>>` block works fine
# after a single `some {}` clause, but not after `some {} or some {}`) --
# this rule has no custom message for that reason, matching the asg
# precedent's own choice, not an oversight.
#
# Verified directly against cfn-guard 3.2.0 with hand-built fixtures
# before this file was written into the task tree (all four outcomes
# checked, `cfn-guard validate`, real exit codes):
#   - wired only (LoggingConfig.LogGroup Ref to an arbitrarily-named log
#     group, e.g. "/platform/event-processor", no /aws/lambda/ name
#     anywhere) -> PASS (exit 0). This is the shape the pre-fix rule
#     scored 0.0 -- the bug this fix closes.
#   - convention-name-only (log group literally named
#     "/aws/lambda/event-processor", no LoggingConfig.LogGroup) -> PASS
#     (exit 0). This scenario's own reference solution's shape.
#   - neither (log group named "/platform/event-processor", no wiring) ->
#     FAIL (exit 19). This is catch 4's own awscdk-arm broken fixture
#     shape -- see solution/broken/log-group-name-diverges-from-function/
#     under the generated awscdk task dir.
#   - no log group at all (function exists, nothing else) -> FAIL
#     (exit 19). Redundant with the tier-0 `log-group-exists` check by
#     design (fail-closed), same convention as this file's own
#     `functions_exist_but_no_log_groups` rule below.
#
# RESIDUAL GAP (narrower than the pre-fix state -- see
# specs/lambda-log-group-ownership-and-retention.yaml's own header
# comment for the full accounting): an agent who names an UNWIRED log
# group with the RIGHT PREFIX but the WRONG SUFFIX (e.g.
# "/aws/lambda/processor" when the function is "event-processor") still
# evades this rule on awscdk specifically -- both the plain-string and the
# `Fn::Join`-shaped naming disjuncts below only check the static
# `^/aws/lambda/` regex, since cfn-guard has no way to compute this
# function's own expected suffix dynamically. This is why catch 4's
# awscdk-arm broken fixture uses a name that fails the PREFIX check
# outright (a wholly unrelated name), not merely a wrong-suffix one -- it
# stays within what this rule can actually prove.
# hcl_raw and terraconstructs close this gap fully via Rego's exact-match
# string comparison (oracles/rego/lambda-log-group-ownership-and-
# retention/policy.rego).
#
# 2026-08-21, ROUND 2 (second adversarial-review fix, same day, on top of
# the round above -- NOT a separate authoring pass): the rule as first
# hand-authored matched `Properties.LogGroupName` only as a PLAIN STRING.
# A genuinely correct solution that names the log group
# `/aws/lambda/${fn.functionName}` by TypeScript template interpolation
# off the function's OWN name (rather than a hardcoded literal) --
# functionally identical to, and no less correct than, this scenario's own
# reference solution's literal-name shape, and the IDENTICAL authoring
# idiom the hcl_raw reference solution itself uses
# (`tasks/anchor/lambda-log-group-ownership-and-retention-hcl-raw/
# solution/solve.sh`: `name = "/aws/lambda/${aws_lambda_function.
# event_processor.function_name}"`, accepted there) -- was scored 0.0 here,
# because aws-cdk-lib 2.263.0 (this arm's own pin) synthesizes a template
# expression like that as an `Fn::Join` intrinsic object
# (`{"Fn::Join":["",["/aws/lambda/",{"Ref":"EventProcessorD4153CB5"}]]}`),
# never a plain string, and cfn-guard's `==` against a string literal never
# matches an object. This was the exact "alternative shape scored wrong"
# failure this batch exists to avoid (docs/adding-scenarios.md §1 item 3a)
# -- and arm-asymmetric, since the identical idiom was already accepted on
# hcl_raw. Fixed by adding the second `some %log_groups { ... }` disjunct
# below, which reaches into the `Fn::Join`'s own argument list
# (`.'Fn::Join'[1][0]`, the first element of the joined-strings array --
# CDK always emits the literal prefix segment first, then the `{"Ref":...}`
# to the function, for this exact interpolation shape) and applies the
# SAME prefix regex to it. Verified directly against cfn-guard 3.2.0,
# 2026-08-21, before this file was re-saved: a real `cdk synth` of
# `logGroupName: \`/aws/lambda/${fn.functionName}\`` (aws-cdk-lib
# 2.263.0, reproduced from the rejected-work reproduction stack) now PASSes
# (previously FAILed, exit 19, `ComparisonError ... PathAwareValues are not
# comparable map, Regex`); six hand-built fixtures re-checked against the
# fixed rule for regression -- wired-only PASS, convention-literal-only
# PASS, convention-Fn::Join-only PASS (the new case), unrelated-literal-
# name-no-wiring FAIL, unrelated-Fn::Join-name-no-wiring FAIL (confirms the
# new disjunct does not overmatch -- a differently-prefixed Fn::Join still
# fails it), no-log-group-at-all FAIL. This gap does not exist on
# hcl_raw/terraconstructs at all: Rego reads `.values.name` from
# `terraform show -json`, always a fully-resolved plan-time-known string
# (verified directly, this spec's own header comment deviation 2) -- there
# is no HCL equivalent of an un-evaluated `Fn::Join` surviving into plan
# output.

let functions = Resources.*[ Type == "AWS::Lambda::Function" ]
let log_groups = Resources.*[ Type == "AWS::Logs::LogGroup" ]

rule log_group_governs_the_function when %functions !empty {
	some %log_groups {
		Properties.LogGroupName exists
		Properties.LogGroupName == /^\/aws\/lambda/
	}
	or
	some %log_groups {
		# CDK renders `logGroupName: \`/aws/lambda/${fn.functionName}\``
		# (template interpolation off the function's OWN name, the same
		# authoring idiom the hcl_raw reference solution uses via HCL's
		# `${...}` interpolation) as an `Fn::Join` intrinsic, not a plain
		# string -- `{"Fn::Join":["",["/aws/lambda/",{"Ref":"..."}]]}` --
		# so the plain-string disjunct above never matches it even though
		# the rendered name IS the convention name. This disjunct accepts
		# that intrinsic shape by checking its own first joined segment
		# against the same prefix regex. See "2026-08-21, ROUND 2" below.
		Properties.LogGroupName.'Fn::Join'[1][0] exists
		Properties.LogGroupName.'Fn::Join'[1][0] == /^\/aws\/lambda/
	}
	or
	some %functions {
		Properties.LoggingConfig.LogGroup exists
	}
}

# Fail-closed: a Lambda function exists but no log group exists anywhere in
# the template at all -- redundant with the tier-0 `log-group-exists`
# check by design (mirrors oracles/cfn-guard/s3-lambda-log-retention/
# policy.guard's own fail-closed convention); never changes an OBSERVED
# tier for catch 1's own fixture (tier-0 fails first), kept here only so
# this policy is self-consistent on its own.
rule functions_exist_but_no_log_groups when %functions !empty {
	%log_groups !empty
}
